#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064fbd9c-80ed-51b0-98cb-32bd23adfd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308800} {:answer/id #uuid "131e40fb-788f-5311-aa90-2ba7a9c2a470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308884} {:answer/id #uuid "604d06b0-499e-5ae6-b383-8e4f9aff6004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309476} {:answer/id #uuid "7489e527-2be8-5430-b0a7-0c197eae2f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309652} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed2b579-2a1c-5028-941b-30fd3eb330b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309738} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b15e461-76ed-5fe3-95c8-709b91e6e409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "169c8d2a-baf7-5d16-8418-7c154f48a72f" :question/math-academy-id "q-335578" :question/problem "Throughout an experiment in a laboratory, a culture contains $1\\,200$ bacteria in total (limited by nutrients and space). At the beginning of the experiment, $200$ of the $1\\,200$ bacteria are resistant to a new antibiotic. After $4$ hours, the number of resistant bacteria increases to $500.$ To the nearest hour, how long does it take for the number of resistant bacteria to reach $900?$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of resistant bacteria, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in hours.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the resistant bacteria population, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total bacteria population in the culture and its current resistant population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total bacteria population the culture can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,200$ bacteria, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,200-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,200-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,200-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,200-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,200-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,200-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial population of resistant bacteria was $200$ cells. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=200,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K{e}^{0} & =200 \\\\ 1\\,200-K & =200 \\\\ K & =1\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $4$ hours, the resistant bacteria population was $500$ cells. So, applying the condition $P(4)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-4k} & =500 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,000{e}^{-4k} \\\\ {e}^{-4k} & =\\frac{700}{1\\,000}=\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ -4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $900$ bacteria are resistant to the antibiotic. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =900 \\\\ 1\\,200-1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =900 \\\\ 300 & =1\\,000{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{300}{1\\,000}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $900$ bacteria will be resistant to the antibiotic after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right)}{-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right)}=13.502…≈14\\,\\text{hours},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9bd300d-3cde-5115-b933-4a186627d22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313423} {:answer/id #uuid "c936496b-a58c-5b8f-b320-be0007adee30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313545} {:answer/id #uuid "d963bd98-724f-537c-a5ca-76474c2d95fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313684} {:answer/id #uuid "db528e71-baab-5111-a2af-c0a2bacba91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313698} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ea987b-2139-54ee-97a2-75367b404733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313698} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba8873aa-ed41-5e1c-ba53-518a94054311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2bc6ca00-9c74-5560-90c3-3edc0b06719c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339591" :question/problem "The mass of a radioactive sample $R(t)$ (in milligrams) after $t$ days can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nIf the sample initially had a mass of $80$ milligrams, calculate its mass after $30$ days. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We model the mass of the sample using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}t}=-0.05R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nR(t)={R}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${R}_{0}$ is the initial mass.\n\nWe're told that the initial mass of the sample is $80$ milligrams, so we have ${R}_{0}=80.$\n\nTherefore, the mass of the sample $R$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nR(t)=80\\,{e}^{-0.05t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the mass of the sample when $t=30,$ we substitute $t=30$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(30) & =80\\,{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 30} \\\\  & =80\\,{e}^{-1.5} \\\\  & =17.850…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the mass of the sample after $30$ days is $18$ milligrams."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a7e41f1-21b5-51bc-a645-510915ab4711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312300} {:answer/id #uuid "33d9ce25-7a2a-5f5b-9154-98c3a14ea6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312392} {:answer/id #uuid "560d32b6-eda3-5813-b354-a2a98296536a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312668} {:answer/id #uuid "9c21cbc2-e8d5-5b4f-8ffc-290d12f4de2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "46\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313210} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a8f37c-e4a4-5f8d-afc5-ccf8750112c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313210} :answer-field/id #uuid "d810adbf-f092-5786-afe1-50c90da85099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5a9b5944-9f23-58ad-9832-a16d905d27ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-339650" :question/problem "The population of a bacterial culture $N=N(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, and $N(3)=2\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." :question/worked-solution "We model the population of the bacterial culture using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial population.\n\nWe're told that the culture initially contained $500$ bacteria, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the population $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=2\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 2\\,000 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{2\\,000}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ k & =0.462\\,09…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈46\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158b0545-6f5a-5a4d-b4ca-f6f2be014a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312124} {:answer/id #uuid "3c219143-5094-5f28-96ec-4ed729172ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312462} {:answer/id #uuid "9768d56c-223b-53f2-ab00-6a61a2ef2fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313179} {:answer/id #uuid "bbab99f4-d0ae-5279-b113-52dd0382a84f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313439} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd1d746-167e-5883-8194-9ead0c37e961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313179} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f09ace-20ad-5f70-a9d1-59859d96853d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a50ab43-25d2-5145-8f23-9157e10a1e9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339651" :question/problem "The value of a savings account $A=A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If the account initially had $\\$12\\,000,$ and $A(4)=\\$15\\,000,$ calculate the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." :question/worked-solution "We model the value of the savings account using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=kA.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the account initially had $\\$12\\,000,$ so we have ${A}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $A(4)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(4) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 4} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{4k} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =1.25 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{4k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.25) \\\\ k & =0.055\\,78…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈6\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cfd52e-1fc1-51eb-85e5-32d9d3dc6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313486} {:answer/id #uuid "f37132fc-0cae-54dc-850c-42a615eac8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524810} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fd73eaf-0df8-5718-b843-1cd77b6090a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8593de99-5055-5455-bd33-e38ccd6c9851" :question/math-academy-id "q-340496" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=8x+{e}^{-3x+5}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{-3x+5}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+{e}^{-3x+5}) \\\\  & =8-3{e}^{-3x+5} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "970e1936-ac2b-534a-8ed4-97d799d35e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313177} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a75775-f14f-5a3f-b4e1-1d6d0f134ccc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8caad42a-7778-5faa-ba43-6f75823507a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340497" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=-4{e}^{2-x}$ at the point where $x=2-ln⁡(3).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u},\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2-ln⁡(3)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2-ln⁡(3)} & =4{e}^{2-(2-ln⁡(3))} \\\\  & =4{e}^{ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db185f31-d898-509f-b9c4-a2881a9060fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28(1-7x)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} {:answer/id #uuid "0367ff3f-be48-44c0-9200-bace643c1760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28\\left(1-7x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313696} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ad8472-0eaf-547f-a51d-d9fd88b94c6d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45dae178-5aad-56cb-99e8-4cae2859e574" :question/math-academy-id "q-340690" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(1-7x)^{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=1-7x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-7.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =-28(1-7x)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315037 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "804c89d6-ac48-5950-9eea-d3b372fd6ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313008} {:answer/id #uuid "b4127a04-7701-46a5-a160-b1ec59409c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12e^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313008} :answer-field/id #uuid "54daf3d6-e597-5f82-adf6-49dc58401548" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "85e8601e-9018-5cbf-862d-85832e302cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-340949" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{4{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =12(1)^{2}{e}^{4(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =12{e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315038 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa1743b3-cca5-5571-9ea3-78db4f0b711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313309} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5d74d6-24a0-4c51-b92d-8c37e51d12f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\ln5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313309} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3811faf-1cb9-5232-9104-02c745e33d3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d403927-9339-566e-9390-9903483d0c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-340969" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{x-{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (1-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5))|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-3(1)^{2}){5}^{1-(1)^{3}}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {5}^{0}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dddace1-6902-5bd8-8d9c-4f8e9ee4c695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316001} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb310ac7-3d59-5c34-90b1-fa1d08886eef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5911b4d-cb67-58a7-a1eb-0368289d94d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-342630" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{2}+2x+1)}^{2},$ the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{2},\\,g={x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2g\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =[2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(2x+2){]}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2((0{)}^{2}+2\\cdot (0)+1)(2\\cdot (0)+2) \\\\  & =2(1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317308 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afdc11cd-82dd-5c6f-bcb3-29722512bae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316117} :answer-field/id #uuid "466922a0-7e46-579f-8acd-8a627c38b832" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4d9eda9c-9861-5891-97dc-ba4b870f7db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342663" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(3x+1)}^{4},$ the slope at the point $(-1,16)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{4},\\,g=3x+1\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=4{g}^{3},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =4{g}^{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12(3x+1{)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=-1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & =[12(3x+1{)}^{3}{]}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =12(3\\cdot (-1)+1{)}^{3} \\\\  & =12(-2{)}^{3} \\\\  & =-96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317312 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3cdb155-0bd1-58bc-961e-1bfa7707dad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217} {:answer/id #uuid "6547e4e5-5934-4e34-acb1-3afb5ae5bf10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(x-3\\right)}{\\sqrt{x^2-6x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316217} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd768cbb-f716-59e5-acc7-6f058b68d4ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1f8a23e9-b484-592c-83ed-60ed3cc8b897" :question/math-academy-id "q-342680" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u={x}^{2}-6x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-6.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (2x-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-6x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317313 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8316ace3-a7b2-57fb-a2e0-d20a2908d622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315844} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c696cc-7f33-5839-8d2a-38d27e70f63c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c93d262c-d38e-53b1-ba9c-9af520b2e82c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342683" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{3x+6}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3x+6.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317321 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0b074e-9124-53da-b940-4a0beab23775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316419} {:answer/id #uuid "4abe05c3-0f06-52ad-8909-cbe4491128f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2(5x+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524509} {:answer/id #uuid "41e5e3a5-9d36-48a7-bc0f-19bdd775286f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576469} {:answer/id #uuid "9562bb93-b2ba-493b-b623-c0b002ef200c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576470} {:answer/id #uuid "79ba6767-590d-47b0-8fdc-a86a2e93a19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac52\\left(\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576471} {:answer/id #uuid "24c1f001-0d0c-4e27-9152-530789816a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac52\\left(5x\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576472} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bdc8ea-ee8f-4bfc-8c2b-63128b70751d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac52\\left(5x+1\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186576473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524509} :answer-field/id #uuid "cae0bf8f-009f-5cd9-a958-5250470d215a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65a56fb3-c803-55e2-851b-d52f4cc805e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-342764" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5x+1}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5x+1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{(5x+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22c57943-65ff-5d5d-81f8-1002a59bb417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315311} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7bce52-e2d7-50a5-a44d-84c8a8cc1d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "febc6c25-71ff-51d9-b851-6f19e65ebcd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342802" :question/problem "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+12x}}$ at the point where $x=2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=3{x}^{2}+12x.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x+12.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (6x+12) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={\\left(-\\frac{6x+12}{2\\sqrt{(3{x}^{2}+12x{)}^{3}}}\\right)|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6(2)+12}{2\\sqrt{(3(2{)}^{2}+12(2){)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{24}{2\\sqrt{{36}^{3}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc8b5aee-000a-5a8d-818f-e20c5debd3ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316184} {:answer/id #uuid "7c419ad2-83cf-57c7-b5a2-d7c1c6a8cf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20(4x+3)^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541677} :answer-field/id #uuid "625a79d7-54d7-570b-aa7c-729d582ac660" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ebc59fe-5ccc-59b1-9757-9c760459c2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342804" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={(4x+3)}^{5},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=4x+3$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{5}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{5})^{′}=5{g}^{4}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(4x+3)^{′}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =5{g}^{4}\\cdot (4) \\\\  & =20(4x+3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8817f05b-018c-5853-b6cc-e00880617a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315880} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ab2d60-fecd-56b2-a6fc-370a03381af0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2fb4db78-694f-5845-af11-624db7bcff81" :question/math-academy-id "q-342875" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{4}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})^{′}=5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{5}-4{x}^{4})(\\,5{x}^{4}-16{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54037730-2600-58cb-b40d-648fd184a076" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315600} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f9abe6a-bb3b-59b2-9f07-73fdde4dcc36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a140a75-4d09-557b-baa7-d7202838dd2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342883" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\sqrt{5x-4},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=5x-4$ and $f(g(x))=\\sqrt{g(x)}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={(\\sqrt{g})}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(5x-4)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{g}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{5x-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d665e027-6ab2-5ec3-82b7-84f4dddc219b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-4{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316315} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1297ade-d13c-5373-bff2-2ee35469ee63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49fe03e2-8c48-5c16-b76a-8505b327ec2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342891" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5}},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5$ and $f(g(x))=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g(x)}}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{g}}\\right)}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{′}=8{x}^{3}-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{g}^{3}}}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}+5{)}^{3}}}(\\,x-4{x}^{3}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e15c4f3-e0aa-5296-8996-2def04133195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6x+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316002} :answer-field/id #uuid "adb706a4-b97c-5d9a-a3d9-b9ec8f4ae7e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f16d8b27-8720-58fb-ad21-d69be0217bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342910" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=(3-2x{)}^{3},$ the equation of the tangent at the point $(2,-1)$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule to find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\nFirst, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf={g}^{3},\\,g=3-2x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=3{g}^{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-6(3-2x{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line at the point where $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & =(-6{(3-2x)}^{2}){|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =-6{(3-2(2))}^{2} \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(2,-1)$ is $m=-6.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-6\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ y & =-6x+12-1 \\\\ y & =-6x+11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d8e18d-55da-5747-a381-b32f0cd64912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315254} {:answer/id #uuid "2663e8c9-ddca-5ed4-a0c7-4cf541120ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k{F}^{2}(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315335} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1c60c4-ae35-5c6d-b92a-7fc0de21cd3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=kF(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315644} {:answer/id #uuid "653fb905-e4dc-5614-96c4-2effafd1ebcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} {:answer/id #uuid "a062595a-6ef4-5175-b64d-a91182de7ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=-k(100+F)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316021}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315681} :answer-field/id #uuid "360d624d-1168-5448-a7ea-9e7a2bd530a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2bd5b32-0a6c-5631-90f3-4c8beb909e62" :question/math-academy-id "q-344214" :question/problem "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $100$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=100\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(100-F).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317473 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0259fd14-c9a9-58b6-a404-bff6dbf7badd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315128} {:answer/id #uuid "2b505a49-68dc-5562-bdf8-5c978ed54f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315372} {:answer/id #uuid "71a08b4a-f491-5881-ac62-23a6895de4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k{S}^{2}(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315763} {:answer/id #uuid "8331eb98-12ad-5b45-8ce6-0bb6ebdd5d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500-S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315845} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5b4005-e626-5fa7-abfe-28e258dd834a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-k(500+S)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315128} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fa6037-cb1e-50be-a0f9-38bac3fc52f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab916584-a26d-54f0-907f-592aaa033fea" :question/math-academy-id "q-344218" :question/problem "A school administrator observed that the growth of the number of students enrolled in a program is directly proportional to the difference between the program’s maximum enrollment capacity and the current number of students enrolled. Let $S(t)$ be the number of students enrolled, measured in students, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of students the program can support is $500$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the number of students enrolled in the program? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of students enrolled $S(t)$ in the program changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of students the program can support and the current number of students enrolled. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-S),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of students the program can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=500\\text{ students},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-S).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f087d09-e1e7-5ab8-982f-cbc3540e7119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbca73a-2595-5694-b56c-f975654c4d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=kP(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316199} {:answer/id #uuid "d2316d69-8166-551c-a84b-3d2353b842a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316296} {:answer/id #uuid "df79cf0d-10f8-59a7-8669-02f7ce1dd947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k{P}^{2}(240-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316354} {:answer/id #uuid "e28e46f1-8fff-5681-9163-603d3ff9e8da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(240+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316374}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209} :answer-field/id #uuid "64f84fa9-a2f0-5ca4-b07d-e3fc3dc62fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54e7291f-5b5b-5bf3-a3dd-9eac24a3f4e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344219" :question/problem "An engineer observed that the growth of the number of solar panels installed at a facility is directly proportional to the difference between the facility’s maximum number of solar panels and the current number installed. Let $P(t)$ be the number of solar panels, measured in panels, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support is $240$ panels, which differential equation could be used to model the number of solar panels installed? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of solar panels $P(t)$ installed changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support and the current number of solar panels. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of solar panels the facility can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=240\\text{ panels},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(240-P).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a6efe89-1d82-5203-8a6a-24c18502b095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(80-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315360} {:answer/id #uuid "56b622c6-6784-5363-8e24-c96bb51d0957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(35-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315619} {:answer/id #uuid "8251af8d-948e-5507-b3a9-8ad30939c31a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(25-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315838} {:answer/id #uuid "996235ec-ab0f-5b92-84a3-56a94cd4f991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(50-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315975} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c1553-bd69-57f7-ac0c-a7f412f84db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dB}{dt}=k(18-B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315975} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05a2153-5b63-5b4c-9cfe-238a0120df42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "999dd29f-4bf1-52fd-b7b1-1ef128e903af" :question/math-academy-id "q-344228" :question/problem "A laboratory incubator initially contains $18$ bacterial cultures. The number of cultures in the incubator $B(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in days. If the maximum capacity of the incubator is $50$ cultures, which differential equation could be used to model the number of cultures? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of cultures in the incubator obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of cultures changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of cultures in the incubator. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-B),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the incubator, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=50$ cultures, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}B}{\\text{d}t}=k(50-B).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11749536-a0f7-5d13-b256-6fdeb5b98399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(320-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315223} {:answer/id #uuid "151ae25d-d64b-5851-adfb-806b30416fbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(800-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315244} {:answer/id #uuid "5a620149-4b03-5b37-bbf4-472c6b574ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1000-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} {:answer/id #uuid "66e6db5c-1814-50aa-8ce2-db2edeec9f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315692} {:answer/id #uuid "c799b39e-df7d-5d1d-bea3-881572cd85f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dC}{dt}=k(1500-C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315631} :answer-field/id #uuid "393ad745-9bcc-5868-a810-b633bf129cee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3974627f-3801-52ea-b93f-3d1a3033e2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-344233" :question/problem "A data center initially contains $320$ computers. The number of computers in the data center $C(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the data center is $1000$ computers, which differential equation could be used to model the number of computers? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of computers in the data center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of computers changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of computers in the data center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-C),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the data center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1000$ computers, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=k(1000-C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99c790f-778d-5a5f-97a8-2b99e8f49b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318143} :answer-field/id #uuid "877e6d0c-39d3-53e7-b2ef-a30f289e7b59" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cb49f682-fb8a-5bf1-935c-970f0a98d037" :question/math-academy-id "q-347842" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $8x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(2)=5.$\n\n$y=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,+${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{x}^{2}+8x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-{x}^{2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(2)=5$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}+C \\\\ 5 & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+4({2}^{2})+C \\\\ 5 & =-\\frac{8}{3}+16+C \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{40}{3}+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}-\\frac{25}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadf5567-9e34-5988-9b93-ee86c9c672c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318296} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b5a75f-9489-53f8-a2ef-2d765ab6e701" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dfecf65-ff44-5a31-9ad0-4e0918756475" :question/math-academy-id "q-347853" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}=0$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =2x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2(0)+C} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{4x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320759 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fe73586-88cc-5eb9-b551-265a21f8d21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2{x}^{3}-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319625}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319625} :answer-field/id #uuid "138ff64c-aba6-5818-815e-c8ab6507d894" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320202}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e6d2e79-6130-55ee-82e5-9a200a11282e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350957" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y}=6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}$ that satisfies $y(1)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-y} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}-{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(6{x}^{2}-1){e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =2{x}^{3}-x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot {1}^{3}-1+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+C) & =0 \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{3}-x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203a3079-ee5c-560e-8635-760c7bf1066a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{x-3}+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319489} :answer-field/id #uuid "f74970cc-7b2d-58bc-b6f8-f2dd0e1d894a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9bd28782-0820-5dae-86e8-4965e6266454" :question/math-academy-id "q-352066" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $(x-3)^{2}{y}^{′}=8$ that satisfies the condition $y(4)=1.$\n\n$y=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =8\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\left(-\\frac{1}{x-3}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{x-3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-\\frac{8}{x-3}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(4)=1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{8}{4-3}+C \\\\ 1 & =-8+C \\\\ C & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{8}{x-3}+9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c11b83c6-f179-56e1-8175-cb7b2c642c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{x+1}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319981}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319981} :answer-field/id #uuid "320d7bcf-5061-50ea-841d-459ccafe7cbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c687828d-8060-5328-a166-293d79967235" :question/math-academy-id "q-352068" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\sqrt{x+1}\\,{y}^{′}=4$ that satisfies the condition $y(3)=10.$\n\n$y=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =4\\int (x+1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(2(x+1)^{1/2})+C \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=8\\sqrt{x+1}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(3)=10$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =8\\sqrt{3+1}+C \\\\ 10 & =8(2)+C \\\\ C & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=8\\sqrt{x+1}-6.\n$$"}]}
